content.text:
Map consumes palette.heat and palette.ramp at render. So dark mode needs dark ramp/heat. Simplest API: expose `palette` resolved for current scheme: `usePalette()` returns active palette object? But map-panel/tract-map use `PALETTES[palette]`. We can restructure so PALETTES entries have `light` and `dark` sub-objects with everything scheme-dependent, plus shared label/note/swatches. Then add a helper `useActivePalette()` given settings.

Hmm, but tract-map receives Palette type. Let's design:

```ts
export type PaletteTheme = {
  heat: Heat;
  ramp: [string,string,string,string];
  accent: string;
  accentDark: string;
  ink3: string;
  tab: string;
  vars: Record<string,string>;
};
export type Palette = { label: string; note?: string; swatches: string[]; light: PaletteTheme; dark: PaletteTheme };
```

Then `useSettings()` could return `theme` resolved. But useSettings returns Settings which is used generically. Add a hook `useAppearance()`? Maybe easier: keep `Palette` shape as-is for light, add `darkVars` and `dark` overrides for ramp/heat/accent etc.:

```ts
export type Palette = {
  label: string; note?: string; swatches: string[];
  heat, ramp, accent, accentDark, ink3, tab, vars;  // light defaults
  dark: { heat, ramp, accent, accentDark, ink3, tab, vars };  // night counterparts
};
```

And a resolver `resolvePalette(id, mode)` → Palette "view" with the same fields. Then useSyncExternalStore returns Settings plus resolved palette? The map components need the resolved one. I'll add `usePalette(): PaletteView` hook = based on useSettings().palette + scheme. But scheme needs to be part of Settings state so re-render happens when system changes.

Make Settings include `mode: "system"|"light"|"dark"` and `dark: boolean` (effective, derived, updated on apply). Store current includes both. Then `useSettings()` returns { corners, palette, heat, cost, mode, dark }.

Then add:
```ts
export function paletteFor(id, dark): PaletteView
```
or in settings: `export function usePalette()` reading store. Hmm store's current includes palette id and dark; components can call `PALETTES[palette][dark ? "dark" : "light"]`.

Where does `usePalette` vs inline go? Just export `useActivePalette()` returning `{ id, ...resolved }`? Let me define:

```ts
export type PaletteTheme = { heat; ramp; accent; accentDark; ink3; tab; vars };
export type Palette = { label; note?; swatches; light: PaletteTheme; dark: PaletteTheme };
export function paletteTheme(id: PaletteId, dark: boolean): PaletteTheme { return PALETTES[id][dark ? "dark" : "light"]; }
```

Then map-panel: `const { heat: heatStyle, palette, dark } = useSettings(); const ramp = paletteTheme(palette, dark).ramp;`
tract-map: `heatRamp(layer, layers, paletteTheme(palette, dark), heatStyle)` — heatRamp takes Palette currently; change to PaletteTheme (it uses palette.heat, ramp).

Settings menu swatches: currently uses light swatches; with a night mode, show `dark.swatches`? Palette swatches represent the palette. Add swatches per theme maybe. Simpler: keep light swatches in menu; but in dark mode the palette swatch shows light colors. Could display dark.ramp colors. I'll put `swatches` inside theme? Light swatches include background/border/heat colors. I'll make swatches part of theme and define dark swatches as background, border, heat ramp. That's extra design work but fine.

Now the actual dark theme values per palette.

Existing globals .dark tokens:
--background: oklch(0.145 0 0) ≈ #252525; --foreground: oklch(0.985) ≈ #fafafa; --card: oklch(0.205) ≈ #343434; --muted: oklch(0.269) ≈ #444; --muted-foreground: oklch(0.708) ≈ #b4b4b4; --border: white/10%; --input white/15%.

For palettes, need dark values that keep each palette's hue identity:

classic (blue/amber/red on near-white): dark background #14161A, foreground #E8EAEE, card #1C1F25, muted #23272E, border #333945, muted-foreground #9AA2AF, heat ramp needs brightness on dark. Classic ramp light: #F7D58E #F2B134 #EC7A3F #E0334F. On dark these are fine (bright). heat-cool #2b59c3 is dark blue, invisible on dark background; heat-cool used for legend low end and maybe map. Let me check usages of heat-cool.

